    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe event modeling and the `getPrimaryIdentifier()` indirection are well-placed (the forward-compat requirement is honored by sourcing the value from the entity, and a dedicated event record is the right unit). The problems are in how serialization is done and one small inconsistency.\n\n1. **Hand-rolled JSON serialization is non-idiomatic here (main concern).** `OwnerCreatedEvent.toJson()` plus the 25-line `quote()` escaper (quote/backslash/control-char/`\\uXXXX` handling) reinvent JSON encoding in a Spring Boot project where Jackson is already on the classpath and already used for REST responses. A maintainer of *this* codebase would expect an injected `ObjectMapper`/`writeValueAsString`, not a bespoke `StringBuilder` escaper that now has to be maintained and re-reviewed for correctness. This is exactly the kind of logic that shouldn't live as a new utility when the standard tool is present. Serialize via Jackson (annotate the record or build a small map), and the hand-written escaping disappears entirely.\n\n2. **Serialization responsibility is arguably on the wrong unit.** Baking `toJson()` into the immutable event record couples the data shape to one wire format. Given this is only emitted to the `AUDIT` logger, the serialization could sit next to the emission (in `AuditOwnerCreated`, using Jackson) and keep `OwnerCreatedEvent` a pure value type. Minor compared to (1), but it reinforces the same smell.\n\n3. **`EVENT`/`event()` is bypassed by its own serializer.** The record exposes `event()` returning the `EVENT` constant, but `toJson()` references `EVENT` directly rather than `event()`. Small inconsistency \u2014 a reader has to confirm the two stay in sync. If a derived `event()` accessor is worth having, use it in `toJson()`; otherwise it's dead surface.\n\n4. **Field/source naming mismatch worth a comment at the field.** The record component is `customerCode` but is fed from `getPrimaryIdentifier()` (and the Javadoc notes it will later carry the memberId). The JSON *key* `customerCode` is mandated by the spec, so the component name is defensible, but the gap between the component name and what it actually holds is a latent trap once the memberId switch happens. This is handled in class Javadoc; it's acceptable, just the weakest point of the naming.",
